---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/technical.css';
import { u } from '../i18n';
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  page="technical"
  title={'Architecture · CryptoBAP'}
  description={
    'CryptoBAP is an end-to-end toolchain. It lifts machine code to an intermediate representation, instruments it with leakage contracts, symbolically executes it to obtain event and observation traces, and translates those traces into Sapic+ for analysis with Tamarin, ProVerif and DeepSec.'
  }
>
  <section class="phero grain">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
      <h1 class="phero__title">Architecture</h1>
      <p class="lead phero__lead">
        CryptoBAP is an end-to-end toolchain. It lifts machine code to an intermediate
        representation, instruments it with leakage contracts, symbolically executes it to obtain
        event and observation traces, and translates those traces into Sapic+ for analysis with
        Tamarin, ProVerif and DeepSec.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <!-- ── Pipeline ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Pipeline</p>
        <h2>Six stages, fully automated</h2>
        <p class="prose">
          Each stage has a defined input and output, so the chain can be inspected — or re-targeted
          — at any point.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-wrap">
        <ol class="tl">
          <li class="tl__step" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:0ms">
            <div class="tl__rail" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="tl__dot">
                <span class="tl__n">01</span>
              </span>
              <span class="tl__line"></span>
            </div>
            <div class="tl__body">
              <div class="tl__head">
                <h3 class="tl__name">Reverse-engineer</h3>
                <span class="tag tag--accent tl__tool">Ghidra</span>
              </div>
              <p class="tl__text">
                The selected region of the target binary is disassembled and recovered as assembly
                code, including for closed-source executables.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="tl__step" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:60ms">
            <div class="tl__rail" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="tl__dot">
                <span class="tl__n">02</span>
              </span>
              <span class="tl__line"></span>
            </div>
            <div class="tl__body">
              <div class="tl__head">
                <h3 class="tl__name">Transpile to BIR</h3>
                <span class="tag tag--accent tl__tool">HolBA</span>
              </div>
              <p class="tl__text">
                Assembly is lifted into BIR, a verified binary intermediate representation, giving a
                machine-checked semantics for the code under analysis.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="tl__step" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:120ms">
            <div class="tl__rail" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="tl__dot">
                <span class="tl__n">03</span>
              </span>
              <span class="tl__line"></span>
            </div>
            <div class="tl__body">
              <div class="tl__head">
                <h3 class="tl__name">Instrument</h3>
                <span class="tag tag--accent tl__tool">Scam-V</span>
              </div>
              <p class="tl__text">
                The BIR program is instrumented with a leakage contract that makes the attacker’s
                observations explicit — constant-time, or Spectre-PHT-style speculation.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="tl__step" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:180ms">
            <div class="tl__rail" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="tl__dot">
                <span class="tl__n">04</span>
              </span>
              <span class="tl__line"></span>
            </div>
            <div class="tl__body">
              <div class="tl__head">
                <h3 class="tl__name">Symbolically execute</h3>
                <span class="tag tag--accent tl__tool">Crypto-aware engine</span>
              </div>
              <p class="tl__text">
                The instrumented program is executed symbolically to collect event and observation
                traces, resolving indirect jumps and summarising bounded loops.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="tl__step" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:240ms">
            <div class="tl__rail" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="tl__dot">
                <span class="tl__n">05</span>
              </span>
              <span class="tl__line"></span>
            </div>
            <div class="tl__body">
              <div class="tl__head">
                <h3 class="tl__name">Extract the model</h3>
                <span class="tag tag--accent tl__tool">Sapic+</span>
              </div>
              <p class="tl__text">
                Traces are simplified using live-variable analysis and translated into a Sapic+
                process, a model of every execution path through the region.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="tl__step" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:300ms">
            <div class="tl__rail" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="tl__dot">
                <span class="tl__n">06</span>
              </span>
            </div>
            <div class="tl__body">
              <div class="tl__head">
                <h3 class="tl__name">Verify</h3>
                <span class="tag tag--accent tl__tool">Tamarin · ProVerif · DeepSec</span>
              </div>
              <p class="tl__text">
                The resulting model is discharged to established protocol verifiers, covering both
                trace properties and observational equivalence.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <!-- ── Leakage contracts ────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Leakage contracts</p>
        <h2>Making the attacker’s view explicit</h2>
        <p class="prose">
          A leakage contract states exactly what an attacker observes while the program runs.
          Changing the contract changes the threat model without changing the binary or the protocol
          model.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid--2 gap-top">
        <article class="contract" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:0ms">
          <div class="contract__head">
            <h3 class="contract__name">Constant-time</h3>
            <span class="tag">Architectural</span>
          </div>
          <p class="contract__body">
            The attacker observes the sequence of control-flow decisions and the addresses of memory
            accesses. This captures classic cache and branch-timing channels: any secret-dependent
            branch or secret-derived address becomes visible.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="contract" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:100ms">
          <div class="contract__head">
            <h3 class="contract__name">Spectre-PHT</h3>
            <span class="tag">Speculative</span>
          </div>
          <p class="contract__body">
            The attacker additionally observes execution along mispredicted conditional branches.
            This captures speculative leakage, where the transient path touches data the
            architectural path never would.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <!-- ── Back ends ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Back ends</p>
        <h2>Three verifiers, two classes of property</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid--3 gap-top">
        <article class="card card--hover" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:0ms">
          <h3 class="backend__name">Tamarin</h3>
          <p>
            Unbounded verification of trace properties, with support for stateful protocols and
            equational theories.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card card--hover" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:90ms">
          <h3 class="backend__name">ProVerif</h3>
          <p>
            Automated analysis in the applied pi-calculus for secrecy, authentication and
            observational equivalence.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card card--hover" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:180ms">
          <h3 class="backend__name">DeepSec</h3>
          <p>
            Decision of trace equivalence for a bounded number of sessions — the tool of choice for
            unlinkability and privacy.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <!-- ── Foundations ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="found">
        <div data-reveal>
          <p class="eyebrow">Foundations</p>
          <h2 class="found__title">Composition across languages</h2>
          <p class="prose found__body">
            Real protocol stacks are not written in a single language. CryptoBAP builds on a
            symbolic execution semantics that supports parallel composition of labelled transition
            systems over different atomic types. By treating the Dolev–Yao model itself as a
            symbolic abstraction, the approach removes the need to translate between bitstrings and
            Dolev–Yao terms at every language boundary.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="list-check found__list" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:90ms">
          <li>Composition of symbolic labelled transition systems with deduction combiners</li>
          <li>CSP-style parallel composition of concrete labelled transition systems</li>
          <li>A refinement linking symbolic to concrete semantics</li>
          <li>Framework instantiation for BIR and Sapic, with correctness proofs in HOL4</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <!-- ── Evaluation ───────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Evaluation</p>
        <h2>What the case studies showed</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="eval gap-top">
        <li class="eval__item" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:0ms">
          <span class="eval__n mono">01</span>
          <p>
            An instruction-cache side channel in WhatsApp Desktop enabling social-graph inference.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="eval__item" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:70ms">
          <span class="eval__n mono">02</span>
          <p>
            Unlinkability issues in the BAC e-passport protocol reproduced under microarchitectural
            observations.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="eval__item" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:140ms">
          <span class="eval__n mono">03</span>
          <p>
            Forward secrecy verified, and a known clone attack on post-compromise security
            identified, from models extracted out of the binary.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="eval__item" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:210ms">
          <span class="eval__n mono">04</span>
          <p>
            Security properties verified for WireGuard, TinySSH, RPC-enc, RPC, NSL, CSur, simple
            Hash and simple XOR.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="eval__note" data-reveal>
        The CCS 2023 platform targets ARMv8 and RISC-V machine code and verifies weak secrecy and
        authentication; the CCS 2026 work adds leakage contracts and side-channel analysis.
      </p>
      <div class="next-links" data-reveal>
        <a class="arrow-link" href={u('papers/')}>
          Publications
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            width="15"
            height="15"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.8"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <path d="M5 12h13M13 6l6 6-6 6"></path>
          </svg>
        </a>
        <a
          class="arrow-link"
          href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.11385"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Read the paper — CCS 2026
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            width="15"
            height="15"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.8"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <path d="M5 12h13M13 6l6 6-6 6"></path>
          </svg>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
